{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '49,88p' .pose/adr/2026-11-07-validation-evidence-binds-to-validated-material.md; rg -n 'F[0-9]|immutable|concurrent|restore' .pose/specs/*validation-evidence-material-binding*; git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"rules. A changed engine makes the evidence stale too. Spec facts, review\nbundles, closeout and validation consume the same verdict.\n\nResults without `material_digest` are judged by the existing rule. A scope that\nis already `done` keeps its current exemption. Naming the first differing path\nwould need a stored manifest and is left as a follow-up. The domain returns one verdict\nthat seal, verify, review-check, closeout-check, close, CLI and MCP all consume.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe binding names the committed tree, and validation runs on the working copy.\nA run therefore records it only when the tracked and untracked files outside\nthe administrative records equal HEAD at the start, and it records it as\nunavailable when a tracked file differs from HEAD after the checks. Editing a\ncommitted symlink or submodule inside a class counts as a material change. The\ncontract does not defend against ignored files the project's own ignore rules\nleave out of the tree, against uncommitted edits to a data file that an earlier\ncommit moved into a class, against a concurrent writer that changes and restores\nmaterial during the run, against sparse or skip-worktree checkouts, or against\na run whose delivery graph could not be collected (that run carries no scope\nprovenance either, so the commit-bound rules that always applied to it still do).\nThese are accepted residual risks of a checkout that its operator controls;\nan immutable execution snapshot would close them and is a follow-up.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- A closeout whose later commits are administrative reuses the passing run\n  instead of repeating it.\n- The result schema gains an additive field, a minor change.\n- Adding a class to the list is a contract change that needs its own ADR.\n\nRejected: caching per-check outputs (stale outputs would pass silently);\nconfigurable class lists per instance (an instance could exempt its own code);\nbypassing evidence for any commit that only touches `.pose/` (specs, policy and\nrules there change what the checks demand).\n8:remediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\n244:- The limits listed in the ADR (ignored files, a data file copied or moved into a class and edited uncommitted, concurrent writers, sparse checkouts, a run whose graph could not be collected) are accepted.\n251:Validation evidence binds to the material it validated: each trailer change set carries a `material_digest` over the head state (path, mode, blob) of the paths it names, minus administrative records; a validation run records `material_provenance` per scope; evidence stays current while the digest holds, so commits that only touch indexes, results, state, assessments or review records no longer force the matrix to rerun. Unreadable or shallow material, an uncommitted material change, a legacy recorded set and a mismatched binding all make evidence stale instead of falling back to the commit-bound rules. Review bundle sealing recomputes the digest from the current tree, and a delegated review no longer lets `artifact-check` republish the delivery index inside its sealed checkout. The governance read view cap is recalibrated from 25,000 to 40,000 observations (metadata cap 8 to 16 MiB) because the closeout plan of this corpus observes 26,051 entries. Left out: naming the first differing path, an immutable execution snapshot, copy-provenance tracking.\n264:- Accepted in the ADR: ignored files, a data file copied or moved into a class and edited uncommitted, a concurrent writer that changes and restores material during a run, sparse or skip-worktree checkouts, a run whose delivery graph could not be collected.\n271:- [open] Validate an immutable execution snapshot, which would close the concurrent-writer, sparse-checkout and ignored-file limits of the material binding. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-07)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"agent_message","text":"Both strict validation runs passed: 236/236 checks repository-wide and 233/233 for `pose-mcp`.\n\nI need to correct the transient-edit finding: the prerequisite material-binding ADR explicitly accepts that risk, so I’m withdrawing it as a new blocker. A separate pinned-policy race remains: carry verifies module metadata against HEAD, then rereads it twice from the working copy. An edit between verification and selection can remove a dependency and leave an affected module carried. The edit can remain in place because final cleanliness exempts this metadata file.\n"}}
